<Layout title="Architectural Drawings vs. Construction Drawings | ATECT Architecture" description="Learn the difference between architectural drawings and construction drawings, what each communicates, and why coordinated documentation matters.">
  <article class="article"><header><p class="eyebrow">Design & Documentation</p><h1>Architectural Drawings vs. Construction Drawings</h1><p class="lede">The terms are sometimes used interchangeably, but project teams should understand what information the drawings are intended to communicate.</p></header>
  <section><h2>Architectural drawings</h2><p>Architectural drawings communicate the design: how spaces are arranged, how the building is experienced, and how major elements relate to one another. Depending on the project stage, they may be conceptual, schematic, developed, or prepared for construction.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Construction drawings</h2><p>Construction drawings communicate the information needed to turn the design into a buildable project. They can include plans, elevations, sections, details, schedules, notes, dimensions, and other coordinated information required for the project's scope.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Why coordination matters</h2><p>A drawing set is most useful when its information works together. Dimensions, details, materials, openings, reflected ceiling information, and other documentation should be developed with the overall design and construction requirements in mind.</p></section>
  <section class="callout"><h2>Need construction-ready documentation?</h2><p>ATECT Architecture provides residential and commercial design services, construction documentation, and project coordination for projects in Maryland, Washington, DC, and Virginia.</p><a class="button dark" href="/contact">Start a Project</a></section>
  </article>
</Layout>
